      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_introduction",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Introduction\n\nThe four multiplication tables we have studied so far (0, 1, 2 and 3) match numbers that are shown directly in the Yupana’s lines, as we saw in earlier texts",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "1bb0274d-dc51-4740-ac62-4d157b06ad88",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Yupana (Addition)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "1bb0274d-dc51-4740-ac62-4d157b06ad88",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Yupana (Addition)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "c4c2c00d-7878-42da-9cc4-060cd7f8d225",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Yupana (multiplication)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": "."
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><table id=\"yupana0\" class=\"tawapukllay\"><tr><th colspan=\"4\"><i>Yupana</i></th></tr> <tr id=\"tf1\"><td id=\"tc1f1\"><span class=\"dot five_dot_c1_up\"></span><span class=\"dot five_dot_c1_center\"></span><span class=\"dot five_dot_c1_bottom\"></span><span class=\"dot five_dot_c2_up\"></span><span class=\"dot five_dot_c2_bottom\"></span></td> <td id=\"tc2f1\"><span class=\"dot three_dot_bottom\"></span><span class=\"dot three_dot_up\"></span><span class=\"dot three_dot_center\"></span></td> <td id=\"tc3f1\"><span class=\"dot two_dot_bottom\"></span> <span class=\"dot two_dot_up\"></span></td> <td id=\"tc4f1\"><span class=\"dot dot_center\"></span></td></td></tr></table><p class=\"desc\"><b>Table 1</b>: Representation of a <i>Yupana</i> line according to Poma de Ayala (<htcite0> ; <htcite1>). CSS inspired by <htcite2> source code.</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "a62b520a-f443-4d83-ab51-b99c9a46dab8",
                     "text": "Prem, Dhavit",
                     "page": "pp. 355",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2018",
                        "month": "04",
                        "day": "01"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 0,
                     "uuid": "f5b74f18-0bc4-46d2-8a5c-64dd80b74dd1",
                     "text": "Poma de Ayala, Felipe Guaman",
                     "page": "pp. 361 - 362",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "1615",
                        "month": "02",
                        "day": "13"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "48dd9187-7ecc-4f54-a387-d9ecc2497de3",
                     "text": "W3school",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "01",
                        "day": "27"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2018",
                     "month": "-1",
                     "day": "-1"
                  },
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "1615",
                     "month": "-1",
                     "day": "-1"
                  }
               ],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "In this text, besides studying the multiplication table of five (5)—which also appears in a line of the Yupana—we will study the table of four (4). But first, let’s begin with a reflection:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 1: The Missing Number</b><br /><br />Based on the four tables you have studied, can you find a reason why the number four is not shown directly in the Yupana’s lines?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_multiple_polygon",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Multiples\n\nLet’s start by recalling the general formula used to build multiplication tables",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "72dcaa0f-07e3-41de-a637-c5b2f528dc33",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Understanding the Multiplication Table (2 and 3)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>r</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">×</mo><mi>N</mi></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Formula 1</b>: General multiplication table formula.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "When we used one (1), two (2), or three (3) for *a*, only *N* = 1 gave a result that named another table. With four (4), things change: 4 is also the product of 2 and 2.\n\nWhen this occurs, we say a number is a multiple of another; that is, 4 is a multiple of 2",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "b16d461d-eb7a-484b-a78b-b12336edf3c0",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "Multiple",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": "."
            },
            {
               "text": "### Quadrilaterals\n\nThere are many geometric figures with four sides. In this text, we will focus on two common quadrilaterals: the square (<htcite0>) and the rectangle (<htcite1>):",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "c0c1967a-f27a-4191-82f1-c5e44c44a297",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W., Square",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "3b7542b4-d24a-4708-a890-e2c57fd0f1ae",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W., Rectangle",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"center_notation\"><svg width=\"430\" height=\"130\"> <rect x=\"30\" y=\"0\" width=\"100\" height=\"100\" style=\"fill:lightgreen;stroke-width:3;stroke:none\" /><text x=\"75\" y=\"125\" font-size=\"1.5em\">a</text> <text x=\"140\" y=\"60\" font-size=\"1.5em\">a</text>  <rect x=\"200\" y=\"0\" width=\"200\" height=\"100\" style=\"fill:lightblue;stroke-width:3;stroke:none\" /> <text x=\"410\" y=\"60\" font-size=\"1.5em\">a</text> <text x=\"290\" y=\"125\" font-size=\"1.5em\">b</text></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Chart 1</b>: Representation of a square (left) and a rectangle (right).</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "From **Chart 1**, we can draw the following conclusions:\n\n- Both the square and the rectangle are closed geometric figures with four sides.\n- Their sides meet at right angles (90°), like the corner of a square.\n- The square has all sides equal in length, each measuring *a*.\n- The rectangle, on the other hand, does not have all sides equal. Instead, it has two pairs of equal sides—in this example, one pair has length *a* (like the square), and the other pair has length *b*.\n- The values *a* and *b* can stand for any number we have studied, on any scale. That is why we use letters for them.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ":"
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_four",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Multiplying by Four (4)\n\nBesides the four sides of a quadrilateral, the number four appears in many everyday objects, such as chairs, tables, and Indigenous metates (stone tools used to grind food):",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><img id=\"table\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('table', '0%')\" class=\"imgcenter\"/><b>Figure 1</b>: Metate on display at the Museo Nacional de Antropología, Costa Rica. Photograph taken on <htdate0>, during a museum visit.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2015",
                     "month": "10",
                     "day": "17"
                  }
               ],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "This figure shows a metate in the shape of a jaguar. The jaguar’s back is flat, so the surface can be used for grinding.",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Constructing the Multiplication Table of four (4)\n\nTo build the multiplication table for four (4), we apply **Formula 1** by setting factor *a* to four (4). Here are the results:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div id=\"mParent4\" style=\"margin: auto; text-align: center;\"></div></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Graphical Representation\n\nWe plot the points from the multiplication table and connect them in a chart:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div class=\"htChart\"><canvas aria-label=\"chart\" id=\"chart1\" style=\"width:30em !important; height:20em !important;\"></canvas></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Chart 2</b>: Points from the multiplication table of 4, connected to form a straight line. Chart created with <htcite0>.</div></p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "6efe7632-faed-4080-a5aa-38463d43e6d7",
                     "text": "Chart JS",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "05",
                        "day": "30"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "To reinforce the concept of multiples, we compare the tables of two (2) and four (4) on the same chart:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div class=\"htChart\"><canvas aria-label=\"chart\" id=\"chart2\" style=\"width:30em !important; height:20em !important;\"></canvas></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Chart 3</b>: Points from the tables of two and four, connected to form straight lines. Chart created with <htcite0>.</div></p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "6efe7632-faed-4080-a5aa-38463d43e6d7",
                     "text": "Chart JS",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "05",
                        "day": "30"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Based on what we have studied, we highlight the following from **Chart 3**:\n\n- Except for zero, every product in the table of four is twice the product in the table of two for the same factor. For example, when N = 10: the table of two gives 20 (= 10 × 2), and the table of four gives 40 (= 10 × 4 = 20 × 2).\n- Some numbers appear in both tables. For example, eight (8) is a multiple of both two and four: 8 = 2 × 4 = 4 × 2.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_five",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Multiplying by Five (5)\n\nWhen we talk about repeated addition (multiplication) by five, the first example that comes to mind is probably our hands:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><table class=\"book_navigation\"><tr><td>Left hand</td><td>Right hand</td></tr><tr><td><img id=\"imgLH\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgLH', '0%')\" class=\"imgcenter\"/></td><td><img id=\"imgRH\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgRH', '0%')\" class=\"imgcenter\"/></td></tr><tr></table></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Figure 2</b>: Hands representation. The source code is available on the project’s GitHub (<htcite0>).</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "240800f3-ede4-4deb-9d4e-915a3a03fa68",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Source code",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "05",
                        "day": "30"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2024",
                     "month": "-1",
                     "day": "-1"
                  }
               ],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "The figure shows both of a person’s hands raised, with all fingers extended.",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Constructing the Table\n\nTo build the multiplication table for five (5), we apply **Formula 1** by setting factor *a* to five (5). Here are the results:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div id=\"mParent5\" style=\"margin: auto; text-align: center;\"></div></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Visualizing the Table\n\n**Chart 4** displays the values from the multiplication table of five (5):",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div class=\"htChart\"><canvas aria-label=\"chart\" id=\"chart3\" style=\"width:30em !important; height:20em !important;\"></canvas></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Chart 4</b>: Points from the table of five connected to form a straight line. Generated using <htcite0>.</div></p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "6efe7632-faed-4080-a5aa-38463d43e6d7",
                     "text": "Chart JS",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "05",
                        "day": "30"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Next, we plot the table of five together with the tables we studied earlier:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div class=\"htChart\"><canvas aria-label=\"chart\" id=\"chart4\" style=\"width:30em !important; height:20em !important;\"></canvas></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Chart 5</b>: Points from tables one through five connected, each forming distinct straight lines. Created with <htcite0>.</div></p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "6efe7632-faed-4080-a5aa-38463d43e6d7",
                     "text": "Chart JS",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "05",
                        "day": "30"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Reaching the **table of five** marks the midpoint of our planned study. Before we continue, let’s summarize what we see in **Chart 5**:\n- All tables (except zero) produce points that form upward-sloping lines.\n- Because multiplication is commutative (a × b = b × a), the same product can appear in more than one table.\n- If you look down a column (the same factor), you can see the pattern of each table. We will explore these vertical patterns in the next sections.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "To conclude this study of the table of five (5), we revisit the Yupana’s line (see Table 1). Note that it includes the number five alongside four (4), three (3), two (2), and one (1). So we can say that a single Yupana line shows numbers that are not all multiples of one number.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_nature",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Modeling Nature\n\nWhen we work with squares and triangles on a sheet of paper, we are drawing shapes on a flat surface. However, even a very thin sheet has some thickness, so it is more than a two-dimensional plane",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "0cb685b3-30b9-4c4b-8b8a-cb777c51208a",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Looking at the world around us, we see that even objects that seem to have only two dimensions (height and width) still have depth or thickness. So we need models that come closer to reality, as shown in **Figure 3**:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><img id=\"pyramid\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('pyramid', '0%')\" class=\"imgcenter\"/><b>Figure 3</b>: A pyramid generated using a Python script available in the project repository (<htcite0>).</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "4f5657f4-4284-4e13-b90f-b0f1a7e50c68",
                     "text": "Scripts in Repository",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "10",
                        "day": "27"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "In **Figure 3**, we see a pyramid composed of a square base and four triangular sides.\n\nStructures based on triangles and squares help us model, for example, the roofs of buildings or even mountains like those in Machu Picchu, Peru:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><img id=\"mp\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('mp', '0%')\" class=\"imgcenter\"/><b>Figure 4</b>: Photograph of Machu Picchu, Peru. Image taken on <htdate0>, during a visit to the city.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2017",
                     "month": "8",
                     "day": "16"
                  }
